WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.660
In the summer of 2018, the RAND Corporation
issued a massive study of the Gates Foundation's

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:14.880
ambitious, half-billion-dollar effective teacher initiative.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.529
The findings? Mostly negative. RAND 
concluded that the initiative failed to make a

00:00:19.529 --> 00:00:23.580
dent in student achievement or to
improve teacher quality.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.436
The evaluation teaches some useful lessons.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:31.000
One: the systems were too time-consuming
and the paperwork too burdensome.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:35.180
Reforms tend to fail when they redirect
time from students and learning.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:39.840
Two: even with the new systems in
place, principals continue to inflate

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.200
teachers' scores, because it minimized their hassles

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:49.140
and the reforms did nothing to change that.
Three: school systems were reluctant to meaningfully

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.160
rethink teacher pay. Now, this doesn't
mean we should give up on teacher

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.180
evaluation, but it does mean that
reforming it is much more difficult than

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.540
many once anticipated. The challenge
turns out to be less a willingness

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:07.760
to do it than the ability to do it well. And
that's the lesson worth learning.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:12.540
How do you think we should evaluate teachers?
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:18.380
Also let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:25.120
and be sure to LIKE and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

